From dfc4b343d9286a86944fbefa314875be61181ba8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ableton-wine <local@ableton-wine>
Date: Wed, 12 Aug 2026 18:51:13 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 94/95] winex11: emulate only observed failed pointer warps on
 XWayland

Live implements relative fader and knob dragging by repeatedly calling SetCursorPos to re-anchor the pointer. A visible-cursor XWayland warp can change the virtual X cursor without moving the compositor pointer, so the next cooked motion resumes from the pre-warp point and Live multiplies the physical delta.

Keep the ClickSentinel approach, but do not clear a delta baseline after every warp and do not infer failure from cooked coordinates alone. Correlate the accelerated XI2 raw delta with the next non-synthetic cooked event. Key that evidence by X connection, source, warp generation, and timestamp; conflicts remain native. Auto mode requires two clear failed correlations. Keep the built-in mode disabled pending the desktop and cursor-visibility test matrix, with explicit auto and enabled settings for validation.

Use mutex-protected process-wide state so attached Wine threads share release and cancellation. Suppress every cross-thread synthetic target copy until genuine non-target motion even when the receiving Display cannot match the issuing thread serial. End a sequence on refused warp, button, focus, capture, clip, device, and thread discontinuities; map releases before the middle-drag early return; preserve extended-button bounds; and let final gesture handlers consume native coordinates before warp mapping.

Based on the XWayland warp-emulation approach in ClickSentinel commit d2996582b5d8980feff23d9fd1f8c71480735640.
